REVISION (review pass 2 - cross-codebase verification):

1. NaN-SAFE ORDERING: The BinaryHeap-based top-k search MUST use total_cmp() for float comparison, not partial_cmp(). From cass vector_index.rs (ScoredEntry, lines 459-479):

struct ScoredEntry { score: f32, index: usize }
impl Ord for ScoredEntry {
    fn cmp(&self, other: &Self) -> Ordering {
        self.score.total_cmp(&other.score)
            .then(self.index.cmp(&other.index))  // index as tiebreaker
    }
}

Without total_cmp(), NaN values cause panics in BinaryHeap. Using total_cmp + index tiebreaker ensures deterministic results.

2. TWO-PHASE SEARCH (from xf vector.rs): The search should use a two-phase approach:
   Phase 1: Collect (index, score) pairs only -- NO String allocations
   Phase 2: Look up doc_ids only for the final top-k winners
   This avoids N string allocations for N vectors, only doing K allocations for the K results.

3. PARALLEL SEARCH ENV VAR: Add FRANKENSEARCH_PARALLEL_SEARCH env var (default: true) to let users disable parallel search for debugging. Reference: cass CASS_PARALLEL_SEARCH.

EXTREME OPTIMIZATION ENHANCEMENT (Top-K Vector Search)

## Profile-Informed Optimization for search_top_k

### Opportunity Matrix

| Hotspot                  | Impact | Confidence | Effort | Score |
|--------------------------|--------|------------|--------|-------|
| Two-phase alloc strategy | 5      | 5          | 1      | 25.0  |
| Heap branch elimination  | 3      | 4          | 2      | 6.0   |
| Rayon chunk merge opt    | 4      | 4          | 3      | 5.3   |
| Early abandonment        | 3      | 3          | 2      | 4.5   |
| Bloom pre-filter         | 3      | 3          | 3      | 3.0   |

### 1. Two-Phase Allocation Strategy (Score: 25.0, CRITICAL)

The current design is described correctly in bd-3un.15: "Phase 1 stores only indices+scores (no String allocs), Phase 2 extracts doc_ids for top-k only." This is the single most important optimization. ENSURE it's implemented:

  // Phase 1: Score-only scan (ZERO allocations in hot loop)
  struct ScoreHit { index: u32, score: f32 }  // 8 bytes, fits in register
  let mut heap: BinaryHeap<Reverse<ScoreHit>> = BinaryHeap::with_capacity(k + 1);

  for i in 0..record_count {
      let score = dot_product(...);
      if heap.len() < k || score > heap.peek().unwrap().0.score {
          heap.push(Reverse(ScoreHit { index: i as u32, score }));
          if heap.len() > k { heap.pop(); }
      }
  }

  // Phase 2: String alloc only for top-k (typically 10-20 items)
  heap.into_sorted_vec().iter().map(|h| VectorHit {
      index: h.0.index as usize,
      score: h.0.score,
      doc_id: index.doc_id_at(h.0.index as usize).to_string(),
  }).collect()

This avoids 10,000 String allocations (the doc_id lookup), saving ~200μs for 10K vectors.

### 2. Heap Guard Pattern (Score: 6.0)

After the heap is full (len == k), most candidates will be worse than the worst in the heap. Add a "guard" score to skip the heap comparison entirely:

  let mut min_score = f32::NEG_INFINITY;
  for i in 0..record_count {
      let score = dot_product(...);
      if score > min_score {  // Branch predicted as NOT TAKEN (99%+ of the time)
          heap.push(Reverse(ScoreHit { index: i as u32, score }));
          if heap.len() > k {
              heap.pop();
              min_score = heap.peek().unwrap().0.score;
          }
      }
  }

The key insight: after ~2k vectors, the guard score stabilizes and ~99% of candidates are rejected by a single float comparison (< 1ns). The heap push/pop (~50ns) is almost never reached.

### 3. Rayon Parallel Merge Optimization (Score: 5.3)

For > 10K vectors with rayon parallelism, the merge of per-chunk heaps matters:

  // Each chunk produces a local top-k heap
  // Merge: pour all heaps into one, re-heapify to global top-k

  // BAD: Merge one-by-one
  for chunk_heap in chunk_heaps { global.extend(chunk_heap); }

  // GOOD: Tournament merge (log₂(num_chunks) rounds)
  while heaps.len() > 1 {
      heaps = heaps.chunks(2).map(|pair| merge_heaps(pair[0], pair.get(1))).collect();
  }

Also: set PARALLEL_CHUNK_SIZE to match L2 cache size / vector_bytes:
  - 384-dim f16 = 768 bytes per vector
  - 8MB L2 cache → ~10K vectors per chunk (matches current default)
  - Adjust at runtime: chunk_size = l2_cache_bytes / (dimension * 2)

### 4. Isomorphism Proof

- Ordering: top-k by descending score, ties broken by index (deterministic)
- Two-phase: Phase 2 produces identical doc_ids as single-phase
- Parallel: global top-k identical to sequential (heap is deterministic for same inputs)
- Guard pattern: only changes branch prediction, not comparison result

