Implement the RerankStep that integrates reranking into the search pipeline. This is a composable step that takes top-k candidates and re-scores them for better relevance ordering.

pub struct RerankStep {
    reranker: Box<dyn Reranker>,
    top_k_rerank: usize,           // Default: 100 (only rerank top N)
    min_candidates: usize,         // Default: 5 (skip if too few)
}

impl RerankStep {
    pub fn new(reranker: Box<dyn Reranker>) -> Self;
    pub fn with_top_k(self, k: usize) -> Self;
    pub fn with_min_candidates(self, min: usize) -> Self;

    /// Rerank candidates using a text retrieval function.
    /// The text_fn closure retrieves original text for each document.
    /// Sets rerank_score on each candidate, then re-sorts by rerank_score.
    pub fn rerank<F>(&self, query: &str, candidates: &mut [ScoredResult], text_fn: F) -> SearchResult<()>
    where
        F: Fn(&ScoredResult) -> Option<String>,
    {
        if candidates.len() < self.min_candidates {
            return Ok(());  // Skip: too few to benefit from reranking
        }

        let rerank_count = candidates.len().min(self.top_k_rerank);
        let texts: Vec<String> = candidates[..rerank_count]
            .iter()
            .filter_map(|r| text_fn(r))
            .collect();

        let text_refs: Vec<&str> = texts.iter().map(|s| s.as_str()).collect();
        let scores = self.reranker.rerank(query, &text_refs)?;

        // Apply rerank scores
        for (i, score) in scores.iter().enumerate() {
            candidates[i].rerank_score = Some(*score);
        }

        // Re-sort: reranked candidates by rerank_score (desc),
        // then non-reranked by original score (desc)
        candidates[..rerank_count].sort_by(|a, b| 
            b.rerank_score.unwrap()
                .partial_cmp(&a.rerank_score.unwrap())
                .unwrap_or(std::cmp::Ordering::Equal)
        );

        Ok(())
    }
}

IMPORTANT: ScoredResult does NOT have a .text field (see bd-3un.5). The reranker needs original document text to compute cross-encoder scores. The text_fn closure parameter allows the caller to provide text retrieval (e.g., from FrankenSQLite document store, from file system, or from an in-memory cache). This decouples the rerank step from any specific storage backend.

Design decisions:
- Reranking only the top-k (not all candidates) for performance
- min_candidates threshold skips reranking when too few results (overhead not worth it)
- text_fn closure pattern avoids storing full document text in ScoredResult (memory efficiency)
- Sort stability: use unwrap_or(Ordering::Equal) to handle NaN scores gracefully

