ALIEN-ARTIFACT & OPTIMIZATION ENHANCEMENT (Embedding Job Queue)

## Mathematical Upgrade: From Fixed Threshold to Queueing Theory + Adaptive Rate Control

Current design uses a fixed backpressure_threshold of 1000 pending items. This is ad-hoc and either too aggressive (drops work unnecessarily) or too permissive (allows memory pressure).

### 1. Little's Law for Steady-State Analysis

Little's Law: L = λW (queue length = arrival rate × service time)

At steady state, if embeddings arrive at λ docs/sec and each takes W seconds to process:
  expected_queue_depth = λ × W

For MiniLM at 128ms per doc (batch of 32 → ~4ms amortized):
  If λ = 100 docs/sec, expected queue = 100 × 0.004 = 0.4 (no backpressure needed)
  If λ = 10,000 docs/sec (bulk indexing), expected queue = 10,000 × 0.004 = 40

The OPTIMAL threshold is a function of arrival rate, not a fixed constant.

### 2. AIMD Adaptive Backpressure (Additive Increase, Multiplicative Decrease)

Borrow from TCP congestion control:

  pub struct AdaptiveBackpressure {
      window: AtomicUsize,      // Current admission window
      min_window: usize,        // Floor (default: 32)
      max_window: usize,        // Ceiling (default: 10_000)
      increase_step: usize,     // Additive increase (default: 16)
      decrease_factor: f32,     // Multiplicative decrease (default: 0.5)
  }

  // On successful batch completion: window += increase_step
  // On memory pressure detected: window *= decrease_factor

This automatically adapts to the system's capacity. During bulk indexing, the window opens up. When memory is tight, it contracts. Provably converges to the optimal operating point under stationary conditions.

### 3. Token Bucket for Smooth Rate Limiting

Instead of hard rejection when queue is full, use a token bucket for smooth rate control:

  pub struct TokenBucket {
      tokens: AtomicF64,
      max_tokens: f64,         // Burst capacity
      refill_rate: f64,        // Tokens per second
      last_refill: Instant,
  }

  impl TokenBucket {
      pub fn try_acquire(&self) -> bool {
          self.refill();
          if self.tokens.load() >= 1.0 {
              self.tokens.fetch_sub(1.0);
              true
          } else {
              false
          }
      }
  }

The refill_rate should be set to the measured embedding throughput (self-tuning: measure actual batch processing rate and update).

### 4. Expected Loss Decision for Drop vs Queue

When the queue is near capacity, use expected loss minimization to decide whether to drop or queue:

  L(queue, system_ok) = latency_cost(queue_depth + 1)  // queuing adds latency
  L(drop, system_ok)  = document_value                  // losing the document
  L(queue, system_overloaded) = crash_cost              // OOM / degraded service
  L(drop, system_overloaded) = 0                        // safe, no impact

  action* = argmin_a Σ_s L(a,s) × P(s|queue_depth, memory_usage)

This gives a principled drop policy that accounts for document importance (if available via metadata priority field).

