EXTREME OPTIMIZATION ENHANCEMENT (Benchmarks)

## Profile-Driven Benchmark Design

### Mandatory Baseline Protocol

BEFORE any optimization, capture golden baselines per the extreme-optimization methodology:

  hyperfine --warmup 3 --runs 10 'cargo run --example bench_quick'

### Benchmark Groups with Opportunity Matrices

Each benchmark should compute AND REPORT an opportunity matrix:

  | Function            | p50 (us) | p99 (us) | Budget (us) | Headroom | Priority |
  |---------------------|----------|----------|-------------|----------|----------|
  | dot_product_f16_f32 | 1.2      | 1.8      | 2.0         | 10%      | Low      |
  | search_top_k_10K    | 12000    | 15000    | 15000       | 0%       | HIGH     |
  | rrf_fuse_200        | 45       | 80       | 500         | 84%      | Low      |

Auto-classify: if headroom < 20%, priority = HIGH (at risk of budget violation).

### Regression Detection with Statistical Rigor

Instead of simple "is it faster?", use proper statistical testing:

1. Welch's t-test for paired comparisons (accounts for unequal variances)
2. Effect size (Cohen's d) to distinguish meaningful vs statistically-significant-but-tiny
3. Only report regressions when:
   - p-value < 0.01 AND
   - Cohen's d > 0.5 (medium effect) AND
   - Absolute change > 5% of budget

This prevents false alarm fatigue from noisy CI benchmarks.

### Cache State Awareness

Benchmarks MUST test both cold and warm cache states:

  // Cold cache: drop OS page cache before each run
  sync && echo 3 > /proc/sys/vm/drop_caches  // (requires root)

  // Warm cache: run 3x warmup before measuring
  for _ in 0..3 { search_top_k(index, query, 10); }

Report both. Cold cache numbers are what users experience on first query; warm cache is steady-state.

### Flamegraph Integration

Auto-generate flamegraphs when a benchmark exceeds budget:

  if measured_p99 > budget * 0.9 {
      // Trigger: cargo flamegraph --bench search_bench -- --bench search_top_k_10K
      // Save to benches/flamegraphs/{date}_{benchmark}.svg
  }

### Isomorphism Proofs in Benchmarks

Every benchmark that tests an optimization should VERIFY golden outputs:

  #[bench]
  fn bench_search_with_prefetch(b: &mut Bencher) {
      // Setup: compute golden results WITHOUT optimization
      let golden = search_top_k_baseline(index, query, 10);

      b.iter(|| {
          let results = search_top_k_optimized(index, query, 10);
          // VERIFY: same results as golden
          assert_eq!(results.len(), golden.len());
          for (r, g) in results.iter().zip(golden.iter()) {
              assert_eq!(r.doc_id, g.doc_id);
              assert!((r.score - g.score).abs() < 1e-6);
          }
      });
  }

This ensures optimizations NEVER silently change behavior.

