ASUPERSYNC MIGRATION — CORE TRAIT REVISION (Embedder + Reranker):

The Embedder and Reranker traits gain a Cx parameter for cancel-aware operations:

BEFORE:
  pub trait Embedder: Send + Sync {
      fn embed(&self, text: &str) -> SearchResult<Vec<f32>>;
      fn dimension(&self) -> usize;
      fn is_semantic(&self) -> bool;
      fn category(&self) -> EmbedderCategory;
  }

AFTER:
  pub trait Embedder: Send + Sync {
      async fn embed(&self, cx: &Cx, text: &str) -> asupersync::Result<Vec<f32>>;
      fn dimension(&self) -> usize;
      fn is_semantic(&self) -> bool;
      fn category(&self) -> EmbedderCategory;
  }

  pub trait Reranker: Send + Sync {
      async fn rerank(&self, cx: &Cx, query: &str, docs: &[&str]) -> asupersync::Result<Vec<f32>>;
  }

KEY IMPLICATIONS:
1. embed() is now async — enables cancel-aware Mutex acquisition for ONNX sessions
2. Cx parameter enables: cancellation checks, budget enforcement, tracing
3. Hash embedder: embed() is synchronous internally but async for trait uniformity
   (just wraps the sync computation — zero overhead for fast embedders)
4. Model2Vec: same — synchronous internally, async trait wrapper
5. FastEmbed: genuinely benefits — cancel-aware Mutex lock on ONNX session
6. FlashRank reranker: same — cancel-aware Mutex for ONNX session

RETURN TYPE: asupersync::Result<T> instead of our SearchResult<T>. This enables:
  - Outcome::Cancelled propagation (embedder cancelled = search cancelled)
  - Outcome::Panicked propagation (ONNX crash = graceful RefinementFailed)
  - Integration with asupersync's error recovery actions

OBJECT SAFETY: async trait methods require dyn-compatible async traits.
  Option A: Use #[async_trait] attribute (allocating but simple)
  Option B: Use RPITIT (Return Position Impl Trait In Trait) — Rust 2024 nightly supports this
  Option C: Use asupersync's own async trait pattern
  DECISION: Use RPITIT (Rust 2024 edition supports it natively, no macro needed)

REVIEW FIX — Async trait dyn-compatibility resolution:

PROBLEM: The Embedder and Reranker traits must be:
  1. async (for cancel-aware Mutex acquisition in ONNX embedders)
  2. dyn-compatible (for runtime polymorphism: Box<dyn Embedder>, Arc<dyn Embedder>)

These two requirements conflict because `async fn` in traits produces opaque return types that are NOT dyn-compatible, even in Rust 2024 nightly with RPITIT.

RESOLUTION: Use the `trait_variant` crate (rust-lang official, part of async-wg output) to generate both a static-dispatch and dyn-compatible version:

  use trait_variant::make;

  #[make(SendEmbedder: Send)]
  pub trait Embedder: Sync {
      async fn embed(&self, cx: &Cx, text: &str) -> Result<Vec<f32>, SearchError>;
      fn dimension(&self) -> usize;
      fn is_semantic(&self) -> bool;
      fn category(&self) -> EmbedderCategory;
      fn id(&self) -> &str;
      fn is_ready(&self) -> bool;
  }

This generates:
  - `Embedder` — the base async trait (not dyn-compatible)
  - `SendEmbedder` — auto-generated dyn-compatible variant with Send bounds

Usage:
  - Concrete types implement `Embedder`
  - Generic code uses `impl Embedder` or `T: Embedder`
  - Dynamic dispatch uses `Box<dyn SendEmbedder>` or `Arc<dyn SendEmbedder>`

ALTERNATIVE if trait_variant is not desired: Manual desugaring with BoxFuture:

  pub trait Embedder: Send + Sync {
      fn embed<'a>(&'a self, cx: &'a Cx, text: &'a str) -> Pin<Box<dyn Future<Output = Result<Vec<f32>, SearchError>> + Send + 'a>>;
      fn dimension(&self) -> usize;
      fn is_semantic(&self) -> bool;
      fn category(&self) -> EmbedderCategory;
      fn id(&self) -> &str;
      fn is_ready(&self) -> bool;
  }

This is dyn-compatible and requires no proc-macro, but is verbose. Implementors can use a helper macro to reduce boilerplate.

DECISION: Use `trait_variant` (Option A). It's the Rust async-wg's official solution, minimal dependency, and generates clean code. Add `trait_variant = "0.1"` to workspace dependencies in bd-3un.1.

SAME APPROACH FOR RERANKER:

  #[make(SendReranker: Send)]
  pub trait Reranker: Sync {
      async fn rerank(&self, cx: &Cx, query: &str, docs: &[&str]) -> Result<Vec<f32>, SearchError>;
      fn model_name(&self) -> &str;
      fn max_length(&self) -> usize;
      fn is_available(&self) -> bool;
  }

NON-ASYNC METHODS STAY SYNC: dimension(), is_semantic(), category(), id(), is_ready(), model_name(), max_length(), is_available() all remain synchronous. Only the heavy-compute methods (embed, rerank) are async.

UTILITY FUNCTIONS STAY SYNC: l2_normalize(), cosine_similarity(), truncate_embedding() are standalone functions, not trait methods. They stay synchronous.

