description:
Implement index staleness detection and cache management for the TwoTierIndex. When source data changes but the index hasn't been rebuilt, the system should detect this and optionally trigger a rebuild.

Staleness detection (from xf VectorIndexCache pattern):

pub struct IndexStaleness {
    pub is_stale: bool,
    pub index_modified: SystemTime,
    pub newest_source: Option<SystemTime>,
    pub index_record_count: usize,
    pub estimated_source_count: Option<usize>,
    pub reason: Option<String>,
}

Detection strategies:
1. Timestamp comparison: compare index file mtime with source data directory mtime
2. Count mismatch: compare record_count in index header with count of source documents (if provided by caller)
3. Sentinel file: write a .index_built_at sentinel with build timestamp + source hash
4. Manual invalidation: caller can explicitly mark index as stale

pub struct IndexCache {
    fast: OnceLock<Option<VectorIndex>>,
    quality: OnceLock<Option<VectorIndex>>,
    lexical: OnceLock<Option<LexicalSearch>>,
    data_dir: PathBuf,
}

impl IndexCache {
    pub fn new(data_dir: PathBuf) -> Self;

    /// Get or lazily load the fast-tier index
    pub fn fast_index(&self) -> Option<&VectorIndex>;

    /// Get or lazily load the quality-tier index
    pub fn quality_index(&self) -> Option<&VectorIndex>;

    /// Get or lazily load the lexical index
    pub fn lexical_index(&self) -> Option<&LexicalSearch>;

    /// Check if any index is stale
    pub fn check_staleness(&self) -> IndexStaleness;

    /// Invalidate cache (next access will reload from disk)
    /// Note: OnceLock can't be reset, so this creates a new cache
    pub fn invalidate(self) -> Self;

    /// Get the two-tier index (combines fast + quality)
    pub fn two_tier_index(&self, config: &TwoTierConfig) -> Option<TwoTierIndex>;
}

Sentinel file format (.frankensearch_index_meta):
{
