## Problem

Multiple beads mention asupersync migration in comments, but there is no single authoritative bead defining:
1. How Cx propagates through the API surface
2. When to use asupersync vs rayon vs synchronous code
3. Standard patterns for timeout, cancellation, and structured concurrency
4. Testing patterns with LabRuntime

Without this, each bead will independently reinvent asupersync integration patterns, leading to inconsistency.

## Cx Propagation Rules

### Rule 1: Public async APIs take &Cx as first parameter
```rust
pub async fn search(&self, cx: &Cx, query: &str, k: usize) -> Outcome<Vec<FusedHit>, SearchError>
```

### Rule 2: Synchronous APIs do NOT take Cx
```rust
pub fn embed(&self, text: &str) -> Result<Vec<f32>, SearchError>  // Sync OK
pub fn canonicalize(&self, text: &str) -> String                   // Sync OK
```

### Rule 3: Rayon for CPU-bound data parallelism
```rust
// Vector dot products, batch embedding, SIMD operations
rayon::par_iter().map(|chunk| simd_dot_product(query, chunk))
```

### Rule 4: asupersync for I/O-bound and structured async
```rust
// File I/O, model download, quality embedding timeout, concurrent search phases
cx.region(|scope| {
    scope.spawn(|cx| quality_embed(cx, query));
    scope.spawn(|cx| fast_search(cx, index));
})
```

### Rule 5: asupersync::combinator for composition
```rust
// Parallel: asupersync::combinator::join
// Timeout: asupersync::combinator::timeout
// Race: asupersync::combinator::race (first to complete wins)
```

## Standard Patterns

### Pattern 1: Timeout-Bounded Operation
```rust
match asupersync::combinator::timeout(
    |cx| expensive_operation(cx),
    Duration::from_millis(config.timeout_ms),
).await {
    Outcome::Ok(result) => Ok(result),
    Outcome::Cancelled(_) => Err(SearchError::Timeout { budget_ms }),
    Outcome::Err(e) => Err(e),
    Outcome::Panicked(p) => Err(SearchError::InternalError(format!("{:?}", p))),
}
```

### Pattern 2: Structured Worker Pool
```rust
cx.region(|scope| async {
    for _ in 0..num_workers {
        scope.spawn(|cx| worker_loop(cx, &queue, &embedder));
    }
}).await;
// All workers guaranteed cleaned up after region exits
```

### Pattern 3: Two-Phase Channel
```rust
let (tx, rx) = asupersync::channel::mpsc(capacity);
// Reserve slot, then send (cancel-safe)
let permit = tx.reserve(cx).await?;
permit.send(data);  // Never lost on cancellation
```

## Testing Contract

ALL concurrent tests must use LabRuntime:
```rust
#[test]
fn my_concurrent_test() {
    let lab = LabRuntime::new(LabConfig::new(42));
    lab.run(|cx| async { /* test body */ });
    assert!(lab.quiescence_oracle().is_ok());
    assert!(lab.obligation_leak_oracle().is_ok());
}
```

## Testing
- [ ] Unit: verify all public async APIs accept &Cx
- [ ] Unit: verify sync APIs do NOT accept Cx
- [ ] Integration: LabRuntime determinism (same seed = same result)
- [ ] Integration: timeout cancellation produces Outcome::Cancelled
- [ ] Integration: region cleanup (no orphan tasks after exit)

## Cross-references
- Every bead that mentions "asupersync" should reference this bead for patterns
- bd-3un.24 (TwoTierSearcher) is the primary consumer of these patterns
- bd-3un.27 (job queue) uses structured worker pool pattern

