REVIEW FIX — Hash embedder algorithm reconciliation and entropy fix:

1. ALGORITHM DECISION: The body describes simple FNV-1a modular projection. The ALIEN-ARTIFACT comment proposes JL random hyperplane hashing. These are fundamentally different algorithms with different outputs.

   RESOLUTION: Keep FNV-1a modular projection as the DEFAULT implementation (simple, fast, deterministic, well-tested). The JL/SimHash variant becomes an OPTIONAL mode selectable via constructor:

   pub enum HashAlgorithm {
       /// FNV-1a with modular projection. Default. Deterministic, fast, simple.
       FnvModular,
       /// Johnson-Lindenstrauss random hyperplane projection. Better quality, still fast.
       JLProjection { seed: u64 },
   }

   pub struct HashEmbedder {
       dimension: usize,
       algorithm: HashAlgorithm,
   }

   impl HashEmbedder {
       /// Default: FNV-1a, 384 dimensions
       pub fn default_384() -> Self { Self { dimension: 384, algorithm: HashAlgorithm::FnvModular } }
       /// JL projection with seed for reproducibility
       pub fn jl_384(seed: u64) -> Self { Self { dimension: 384, algorithm: HashAlgorithm::JLProjection { seed } } }
   }

   This preserves backwards compatibility and deterministic regression tests while offering the higher-quality JL variant.

2. XORSHIFT ENTROPY BUG FIX: The ALIEN-ARTIFACT code `bit = (seed >> (j % 64)) & 1` only uses 64 bits of entropy for all dimensions. For 384 dimensions, bits 0-63 repeat ~6 times, creating correlated dimensions.

   FIXED JL implementation:
   fn jl_embed(&self, text: &str, seed: u64) -> Vec<f32> {
       let hash = fnv1a_hash(text.as_bytes());
       let mut vec = vec![0.0f32; self.dimension];
       let mut rng_state = seed ^ hash;  // Combine seed with content hash

       for j in 0..self.dimension {
           // Advance xorshift64 state for EACH dimension (not just shift same seed)
           rng_state ^= rng_state << 13;
           rng_state ^= rng_state >> 7;
           rng_state ^= rng_state << 17;

           // Random sign: +1 or -1
           let sign = if (rng_state & 1) == 0 { 1.0 } else { -1.0 };
           vec[j] = sign / (self.dimension as f32).sqrt();  // 1/sqrt(d) scaling (JL guarantee)
       }
       l2_normalize(&mut vec);
       vec
   }

   Each dimension now gets independent random bits from a properly-advancing PRNG.

3. ASUPERSYNC NOTE: Hash embedding is pure computation (~0.07ms). The async wrapper is trivial:
   async fn embed(&self, cx: &Cx, text: &str) -> Result<Vec<f32>, SearchError> {
       // No cx.checkpoint() needed — too fast to warrant cancellation check
       Ok(self.embed_sync(text))
   }

4. TEST REQUIREMENTS:
   - Deterministic regression: HashEmbedder::default_384().embed_sync("hello world") produces exact same Vec<f32> every run
   - Dimension: output.len() == requested dimension
   - L2 normalization: |norm - 1.0| < 1e-6
   - Different inputs produce different embeddings
   - Empty string produces a valid (non-zero, normalized) embedding
   - Very long input (100KB) doesn't panic or allocate excessively
   - JL variant: different seeds produce different embeddings for same text
   - JL variant: same seed + same text = same embedding (deterministic)
   - JL variant: cosine similarity of random pairs ≈ 0 (orthogonality check on 1000 random pairs, mean < 0.1)

ASUPERSYNC MIGRATION — MODERATE REVISION (Mutex + session pool):

BEFORE:
  - std::sync::Mutex<TextEmbedding> wrapping ONNX session
  - Comment suggests channel-based pool for V2

AFTER:
  - asupersync::sync::Mutex<TextEmbedding> (cancel-aware lock acquisition)
  - For V2: asupersync::sync::Pool<OrtSession> for session pooling (built-in)

KEY CHANGE: asupersync::sync::Mutex is cancel-aware. If a task holding the Mutex is cancelled, the Mutex is properly released (no poison). If a task WAITING for the Mutex is cancelled, it stops waiting cleanly (no deadlock).

REVISED:
  pub struct FastEmbedEmbedder {
      session: asupersync::sync::Mutex<ort::Session>,
      // OR for V2:
      // session_pool: asupersync::sync::Pool<ort::Session>,
  }

  impl FastEmbedEmbedder {
      pub async fn embed(&self, cx: &Cx, text: &str) -> asupersync::Result<Vec<f32>> {
          let session = self.session.lock(cx).await?;  // Cancel-aware
          cx.checkpoint()?;
          // Run inference (CPU-bound, synchronous)
          let result = session.run(inputs)?;
          Ok(result)
      }
  }

  // V2 pool variant:
  impl FastEmbedEmbedder {
      pub async fn embed(&self, cx: &Cx, text: &str) -> asupersync::Result<Vec<f32>> {
          let session = self.session_pool.checkout(cx).await?;  // Cancel-aware checkout
          let result = session.run(inputs)?;
          self.session_pool.return_resource(cx, session)?;  // Return to pool
          Ok(result)
      }
  }

NOTE: ort (ONNX Runtime) is retained — it's the inference engine, not an async runtime. The Mutex/Pool wrapping changes to asupersync's cancel-aware versions.

TESTING: Lab runtime + ContendedMutex for contention testing:
  - asupersync::sync::ContendedMutex tracks lock contention metrics
  - Lab runtime can deterministically reproduce contention scenarios

description:
Implement automatic embedder detection and graceful fallback chain. When a consumer creates a search context, the system should automatically detect which models are available and build the appropriate embedder stack.

Fallback chain (priority order):
1. Quality: MiniLM-L6-v2 (if ONNX model files present)
2. Fast: potion-multilingual-128M (if safetensors files present)
3. Hash: FNV-1a (always available, zero deps)

Availability detection:
- Check model directory for required files (model.onnx, tokenizer.json, etc.)
- Use platform-specific data dirs (dirs crate) for model cache location
- Environment variable overrides: FRANKENSEARCH_MODEL_DIR
- Log which models are available/unavailable at startup (tracing)

pub enum TwoTierAvailability {
    Full,         // Both fast (potion) + quality (MiniLM) available
    FastOnly,     // Only potion available → no quality refinement
    // QualityOnly REMOVED — hash embedder is always available as the fast tier
    HashOnly,     // Only hash → lexical-dominant search, no real semantics
}

pub struct EmbedderStack {
    fast: Arc<dyn Embedder>,
    quality: Option<Arc<dyn Embedder>>,
    availability: TwoTierAvailability,
}

impl EmbedderStack {
    pub fn auto_detect(model_dir: &Path) -> Self { ... }
    pub fn fast(&self) -> &dyn Embedder { ... }
    pub fn quality(&self) -> Option<&dyn Embedder> { ... }
}

This is the 'resolver' that consumers call instead of manually instantiating embedders.

Reference: agent-mail crates/mcp-agent-mail-search-core/src/auto_init.rs (TwoTierContext, TwoTierAvailability)

