ALIEN-ARTIFACT ENHANCEMENT (Embedder Auto-Detection & Quality)

## Mathematical Upgrade: Formal Quality Assessment with Conformal Prediction

The current EmbedderStack auto-detects available models but has no formal quality measurement. Add principled quality assessment that provides distribution-free guarantees.

### 1. Conformal Prediction for Score Reliability

Given a calibration set of (query, relevant_docs) pairs, conformal prediction provides sets C(q) such that:

  P(relevant_doc ∈ top_k(search(q))) ≥ 1 - α

for any α. No distributional assumptions needed. Implementation:

  pub struct ConformalCalibration {
      nonconformity_scores: Vec<f32>,  // Sorted from calibration
      alpha: f32,                       // Desired coverage (default: 0.1)
  }

  impl ConformalCalibration {
      /// Calibrate using a set of (query, known_relevant_doc) pairs
      pub fn calibrate(searcher: &TwoTierSearcher, cal_set: &[(String, String)]) -> Self {
          let scores: Vec<f32> = cal_set.iter().map(|(query, relevant_doc_id)| {
              let results = searcher.search_flat(query, 100);
              // Nonconformity = rank of the relevant doc (lower = better)
              results.iter().position(|r| r.doc_id == *relevant_doc_id)
                  .map(|r| r as f32)
                  .unwrap_or(f32::INFINITY)
          }).collect();
          let mut sorted = scores;
          sorted.sort_by(|a, b| a.partial_cmp(b).unwrap());
          Self { nonconformity_scores: sorted, alpha: 0.1 }
      }

      /// For a new query, how many results to return to guarantee coverage
      pub fn required_k(&self) -> usize {
          let quantile_idx = ((1.0 - self.alpha) * self.nonconformity_scores.len() as f32).ceil() as usize;
          self.nonconformity_scores[quantile_idx.min(self.nonconformity_scores.len() - 1)] as usize + 1
      }
  }

This tells you: "to guarantee 90% probability of including the relevant document, you need to return at least k results." FORMAL coverage guarantee with no distributional assumptions.

### 2. Bayesian A/B Testing for Embedder Comparison (Bakeoff)

Instead of point estimates (avg NDCG), use Bayesian A/B testing with Beta posteriors:

  // For each embedder pair (A, B), on each query:
  //   If A ranks relevant doc higher: A_wins += 1
  //   If B ranks relevant doc higher: B_wins += 1

  // P(A better than B) = P(Beta(A_wins+1, B_wins+1) > 0.5)
  // = regularized incomplete beta function

  // Decision: if P(A > B) > 0.95, declare A the winner
  //           if P(A > B) < 0.05, declare B the winner
  //           else: need more queries (continue testing)

This provides:
- Formal stopping criterion (don't over-test or under-test)
- Probability of correctness (not just "statistically significant")
- Natural handling of ties and near-ties

### 3. Mutual Information for Feature Selection

When choosing which embedder for which query type, compute mutual information:

  MI(embedder, relevance | query_type) = Σ p(e,r|q) log(p(e,r|q) / p(e|q)p(r|q))

This tells you which embedder is most informative for each query type. High MI = the embedder's rankings correlate with actual relevance. Low MI = the embedder adds noise for this query type.

Use this in EmbedderStack to ROUTE queries to the best embedder per query type, rather than always using the same fast/quality pair.

### Implementation Priority

1. Conformal calibration: add as optional constructor on TwoTierSearcher (requires calibration data)
2. Bayesian A/B: add to bakeoff infrastructure (bd-3un.12)
3. MI-based routing: add to EmbedderStack as adaptive mode (requires usage data)

All are optional enhancements that don't change the core API.

REVISION (review pass - FrankenSQLite API verification):

1. TRANSACTION API: FrankenSQLite does NOT have a Connection::transaction() method. Transactions must use raw SQL strings:
   conn.execute("BEGIN")?;
   // ... operations ...
   conn.execute("COMMIT")?;
   // Or on error: conn.execute("ROLLBACK")?;

   The Storage wrapper should provide a transaction() helper that wraps this:
   pub fn transaction<F, T>(&self, f: F) -> SearchResult<T>
   where F: FnOnce(&Connection) -> SearchResult<T>
   {
       self.conn.execute("BEGIN")?;
       match f(&self.conn) {
           Ok(v) => { self.conn.execute("COMMIT")?; Ok(v) }
           Err(e) => { let _ = self.conn.execute("ROLLBACK"); Err(e) }
       }
   }

   For concurrent access, use "BEGIN CONCURRENT" (FrankenSQLite MVCC mode):
   self.conn.execute("BEGIN CONCURRENT")?;

2. PARAMETER BINDING: Connection uses SqliteValue enum for params, not generic impl Params:
   conn.execute_with_params(sql, &[SqliteValue::Text("hello".into())])?;
   conn.query_with_params(sql, &[SqliteValue::Integer(42)])?;
   The Storage wrapper should provide ergonomic helpers that convert Rust types to SqliteValue.

3. ROW ACCESS: Row::get(index) returns Option<&SqliteValue>, not typed extraction:
   let row = conn.query_row("SELECT count(*) FROM documents")?;
   match row.get(0) {
       Some(SqliteValue::Integer(n)) => Ok(*n as usize),
       _ => Err(SearchError::StorageError("unexpected type".into())),
   }
   Consider a typed helper: fn get_i64(row: &Row, idx: usize) -> SearchResult<i64>

4. CONNECTION LIFECYCLE: Connection must be explicitly closed via close() or dropped. No connection pooling. For thread safety, wrap in Arc<Mutex<Connection>> or use one connection per thread.

5. FSQLITE DEPENDENCY PATH: The correct path dependency is:
   fsqlite = { path = "/dp/frankensqlite/fsqlite" }
   NOT fsqlite-core. The facade crate re-exports Connection from fsqlite-core internally.

6. ALSO NEED asupersync: The actual RaptorQ implementation lives in /dp/asupersync, which fsqlite-core depends on. The durability crate may need to depend on asupersync directly for the SymbolCodec trait implementation, or re-use fsqlite-core's abstraction.

description:
TASK: Implement FrankenSQLite FTS5 as an alternative lexical search engine.

FrankenSQLite includes a full FTS5 implementation with BM25 ranking. This task creates an adapter that implements frankensearch's LexicalSearch trait using FTS5, providing an alternative to Tantivy for text search.

WHY FTS5 AS ALTERNATIVE:
1. Single dependency: FrankenSQLite already provides FTS5, no need for separate Tantivy dep
2. Smaller binary: FTS5 is embedded in the SQLite engine vs Tantivy (~5MB added binary size)
3. Transactional consistency: FTS5 index and document metadata are in the SAME database, atomically consistent
4. MVCC: concurrent readers and writers without blocking
5. Simpler deployment: one .db file contains everything (data + FTS index + vector metadata)

TRADE-OFFS vs TANTIVY:
| Feature | Tantivy | FTS5 |
|---------|---------|------|
| BM25 ranking | Yes | Yes |
| Phrase queries | Yes | Yes |
| Prefix queries | Yes (edge n-grams) | Yes (prefix*) |
| Boolean operators | AND, OR, NOT | AND, OR, NOT |
| Tokenizer customization | Full control | unicode61, porter, trigram |
| Performance (search) | Faster (purpose-built) | Adequate (embedded) |
| Performance (index) | Faster (batch-oriented) | Adequate (row-at-a-time) |
| Snippet generation | Via tantivy-highlights | Built-in highlight(), snippet() |
| Binary size impact | ~5MB | 0 (already in FrankenSQLite) |
| Concurrent write | Requires IndexWriter lock | MVCC (fully concurrent) |

ADAPTER API:

  pub struct Fts5LexicalSearch {
      storage: Arc<Storage>,
      table_name: String,  // FTS5 virtual table name
  }

  impl Fts5LexicalSearch {
      pub fn create(storage: Arc<Storage>, config: Fts5Config) -> SearchResult<Self>;

      /// Create FTS5 virtual table (regular content mode, NOT external content)
      /// NOTE: External content FTS5 tables are NOT supported in FrankenSQLite V1.
      /// Use regular FTS5 content tables only.
      /// CREATE VIRTUAL TABLE {name} USING fts5(
      ///     doc_id,
      ///     title,
      ///     content,
      ///     content_preview,
      ///     tokenize='unicode61 remove_diacritics 2'
      /// );
      fn create_fts5_table(&self) -> SearchResult<()>;
  }

  // Implement the same LexicalSearch trait that Tantivy uses
  impl LexicalSearch for Fts5LexicalSearch {
      fn search(&self, query: &str, limit: usize, offset: usize) -> SearchResult<Vec<LexicalHit>>;
      fn index_document(&self, doc: &IndexableDocument) -> SearchResult<()>;
      fn index_batch(&self, docs: &[IndexableDocument]) -> SearchResult<usize>;
      fn delete_document(&self, doc_id: &str) -> SearchResult<bool>;
      fn document_count(&self) -> SearchResult<usize>;
      fn optimize(&self) -> SearchResult<()>;
  }

  pub struct Fts5Config {
      pub tokenizer: Fts5Tokenizer,      // Default: Unicode61
      pub content_mode: Fts5ContentMode, // Default: Stored (regular FTS5 content tables)
      pub prefix_sizes: Vec<usize>,      // Default: [2, 3] (for prefix queries)
  }

  pub enum Fts5Tokenizer {
      Unicode61 { remove_diacritics: bool },
      Porter,                             // English stemming
      Trigram,                            // Substring search (slower but more flexible)
  }

  pub enum Fts5ContentMode {
      Stored,                             // FTS5 stores its own copy (default, recommended)
      Contentless,                        // Index-only (no snippets)
      // NOTE: External content mode is NOT supported in FrankenSQLite V1.
      // Regular FTS5 is fully supported. External content tables (content=, content_rowid=)
      // may be added in a future version if the full FTS5 spec is implemented.
  }

SEARCH QUERY TRANSLATION:
  frankensearch queries need translation to FTS5 syntax:
  - Simple terms: "hello world" -> 'hello world' (implicit AND in FTS5)
  - Phrase: '"exact match"' -> '"exact match"'
  - Boolean: 'cat OR dog' -> 'cat OR dog'
  - Prefix: 'hel*' -> 'hel*'
  - Column filter: 'title:hello' -> 'title:hello'

  FTS5 returns: doc_id, rank (BM25 score), snippet

CONTENT SYNC:
  When using Stored content mode, FTS5 maintains its own copy of document content.
  On document insert/update/delete, FTS5 must be updated:
  - INSERT: INSERT INTO fts5_table(rowid, ...) VALUES (...)
  - DELETE: INSERT INTO fts5_table(fts5_table, rowid, ...) VALUES ('delete', ...)
  - UPDATE: DELETE then INSERT (FTS5 doesn't support in-place update)

  This sync is handled in the document upsert flow (bd-3w1.2).

FEATURE FLAG:
  This adapter is gated behind 'fts5' feature (separate from 'lexical' which is Tantivy):
  [features]
  fts5 = ["dep:frankensearch-storage"]  # FTS5 requires the storage crate (FrankenSQLite)
  lexical = ["dep:tantivy"]              # Tantivy (existing)

  Consumers choose one or both. The fusion layer (RRF) works with either.

File: frankensearch-storage/src/fts5_adapter.rs

