ASUPERSYNC MIGRATION — MODERATE REVISION (persistent job queue):

bd-3w1.3 implements a persistent job queue in FrankenSQLite that replaces bd-3un.27's in-memory queue. The asupersync migration applies to both:

BEFORE:
  - References "crossbeam channels" from bd-3un.27
  - Visibility timeout pattern with manual timer
  - MVCC concurrent workers

AFTER:
  - Worker coordination via asupersync::channel::mpsc (for signaling between queue and workers)
  - Visibility timeout via asupersync::combinator::timeout (cancel-correct)
  - Worker pool via asupersync::sync::Pool or cx.region with multiple scope.spawn()
  - MVCC concurrent access is FrankenSQLite's domain (not asupersync's)

  pub struct PersistentJobQueue {
      db: FrankenSqliteConnection,
      notify: asupersync::sync::Notify,  // Signal when new jobs arrive
  }

  impl PersistentJobQueue {
      pub async fn dequeue(&self, cx: &Cx) -> asupersync::Result<Option<EmbeddingJob>> {
          loop {
              // Check for available jobs
              if let Some(job) = self.try_claim_job(cx).await? {
                  return Ok(Some(job));
              }
              // Wait for notification (cancel-aware)
              self.notify.notified(cx).await?;
          }
      }

      pub async fn enqueue(&self, cx: &Cx, job: EmbeddingJob) -> asupersync::Result<()> {
          self.db.execute(cx, INSERT_SQL, &job).await?;
          self.notify.notify_one();  // Wake waiting worker
          Ok(())
      }
  }

description:
TASK: Implement content-hash based deduplication for embedding pipeline.

Documents are identified by (doc_id, content_hash) where content_hash = SHA-256 of the canonicalized text. This layer prevents re-embedding when content hasn't changed, and detects when content has changed and needs re-embedding.

DESIGN:

  pub struct ContentHasher {
      // Stateless -- just wraps SHA-256
  }

  impl ContentHasher {
      /// Compute SHA-256 of canonicalized text
      pub fn hash(canonical_text: &str) -> [u8; 32];

      /// Compare two hashes
      pub fn matches(a: &[u8; 32], b: &[u8; 32]) -> bool;
  }

  pub enum DeduplicationDecision {
      /// Content unchanged, skip embedding entirely
      Skip { doc_id: String, reason: &'static str },
      /// New document, needs embedding
      New { doc_id: String },
      /// Content changed, needs re-embedding (old embedding invalidated)
      Changed { doc_id: String, old_hash: [u8; 32], new_hash: [u8; 32] },
  }

  impl Storage {
      /// Check if a document needs (re-)embedding by comparing content hashes
      pub fn check_dedup(&self, doc_id: &str, new_hash: &[u8; 32], embedder_id: &str) -> SearchResult<DeduplicationDecision>;

      /// Batch check for dedup (single query, much faster than N individual checks)
      pub fn check_dedup_batch(&self, items: &[(String, [u8; 32])], embedder_id: &str) -> SearchResult<Vec<DeduplicationDecision>>;
  }

INTEGRATION WITH CANONICALIZATION (bd-3un.42):
  The dedup pipeline is:
  1. Canonicalize raw text (NFC, markdown strip, code collapse, whitespace normalize)
  2. If canonical text is empty -> skip (low-signal content)
  3. Compute SHA-256 of canonical text
  4. Check dedup against storage: skip / new / changed
  5. Only if new/changed: actually embed the text

  This means content_hash is computed AFTER canonicalization but BEFORE embedding.
  The hash represents the exact text that was embedded, so identical canonical forms
  always produce the same hash regardless of original formatting.

BATCH DEDUP SQL:
  -- Check which doc_ids need embedding
  SELECT d.doc_id, d.content_hash, es.status
  FROM documents d
  LEFT JOIN embedding_status es ON d.doc_id = es.doc_id AND es.embedder_id = ?embedder
  WHERE d.doc_id IN (?, ?, ?, ...)
  -- Then compare content_hash in application code

PERFORMANCE:
  - SHA-256: ~400MB/s on modern CPUs (sha2 crate with hardware acceleration)
  - For 10K documents with 2KB average canonical text: ~50ms total hash time
  - Batch SQL query: single round-trip regardless of batch size
  - Net savings: skip re-embedding unchanged docs (128ms per MiniLM embed)

WHY SHA-256 (not xxhash or FNV):
  - Collision resistance matters here: two different texts producing the same hash
    means we'd skip embedding, silently serving stale results
  - SHA-256 has 128-bit collision resistance (birthday bound), vs 32-bit for CRC32
  - Performance is not the bottleneck: hashing is 1000x faster than embedding
  - Consistency with FrankenSQLite: ECS objects use BLAKE3 for content addressing,
    SHA-256 is similarly cryptographic-grade

