REPAIR BUDGET FORMULA (from FrankenSQLite repair_symbols.rs):
  slack_decode = 2
  R_formula = max(slack_decode, ceil(K_source * overhead_percent / 100))
  R = min(max_repair_symbols, R_formula)

  For a 73MB FSVI index with 4KB symbols:
  K_source = 73MB / 4KB = 18,688 source symbols
  R = max(2, ceil(18688 * 0.20)) = 3,738 repair symbols
  Repair data size = 3,738 * 4KB = ~14.6MB

DETERMINISTIC REPAIR (from FrankenSQLite):
  The repair symbols are deterministic: given the same source data and config, the same
  repair symbols are always generated. This is achieved by deriving the RaptorQ random seed
  from xxh3_64(source_data). Benefits:
  1. Verification without original: compare generated repair symbols against stored ones
  2. Incremental repair: regenerate specific missing symbols on demand
  3. Idempotent writes: writing the same repair symbols twice is harmless
  4. Cross-replica consistency: any node generates identical symbols

CORRUPTION DETECTION:
  Before invoking the full decode pipeline (expensive), do a quick integrity check:
  1. xxh3_64 of source data vs stored hash: if match, file is intact (fast path)
  2. Per-symbol CRC32: identify which symbols are corrupted (medium path)
  3. Full RaptorQ decode: reconstruct from surviving + repair symbols (slow path)

  The fast path (xxh3_64 comparison) takes < 1ms for a 73MB file.
  Full decode only happens when corruption is detected.

File: frankensearch-durability/src/codec.rs

REVISION (review pass - FrankenSQLite API verification):

1. SymbolCodec LOCATION CONFIRMED: The trait is in fsqlite_core::raptorq_integration (line 334). The beads correctly reference this. The encode() and decode() signatures match exactly:
   fn encode(&self, source_data: &[u8], symbol_size: u32, repair_overhead: f64) -> Result<CodecEncodeResult>;
   fn decode(&self, symbols: &[(u32, Vec<u8>)], k_source: u32, symbol_size: u32) -> Result<CodecDecodeResult>;

2. CodecDecodeResult IS AN ENUM, NOT STRUCT: The decode result has two variants:
   CodecDecodeResult::Success { data, symbols_used, peeled_count, inactivated_count }
   CodecDecodeResult::Failure { reason: DecodeFailureReason, symbols_received, k_required }

   The bead's repair() method should match on this enum, not assume success. Update:
   match self.inner.decode(symbols, k_source, symbol_size)? {
       CodecDecodeResult::Success { data, .. } => Ok(data),
       CodecDecodeResult::Failure { reason, .. } => Err(SearchError::RepairFailed { reason: format!("{reason:?}") }),
   }

3. REPAIR SEED DERIVATION: The bead says "seed = xxh3_64(source_data)". The actual FrankenSQLite API uses:
   pub fn derive_repair_seed(object_id: &ObjectId) -> u64
   This derives from ObjectId (16-byte BLAKE3 truncation), NOT from raw source data. For frankensearch, since we don't have ECS ObjectIds, we should compute:
   seed = xxh3_64(source_data_bytes)
   This is consistent with the bead's approach but uses a DIFFERENT derivation than FrankenSQLite's. Document this difference.

4. REPAIR BUDGET CALCULATION: Use the existing API:
   pub fn select_repair_count(k_source: u32, overhead_percent: u32) -> u32
   This is simpler than reimplementing the formula. The RepairConfig and RepairBudget types are also available for more complex scenarios (object-class-specific budgets).

5. PageSymbolSink/PageSymbolSource: For streaming I/O (large indices), implement these traits instead of loading all symbols into memory. The Sink writes symbols to the .fec file incrementally; the Source reads them back on demand. This keeps memory usage bounded regardless of index size.

   pub struct FecFileSink {
       writer: BufWriter<File>,
       written: u32,
   }
   impl PageSymbolSink for FecFileSink {
       fn write_symbol(&mut self, esi: u32, data: &[u8]) -> Result<()> { ... }
       fn flush(&mut self) -> Result<()> { ... }
       fn written_count(&self) -> u32 { self.written }
   }

description:
TASK: Add RaptorQ repair trailer format to FSVI vector index files.

This extends the FSVI format (bd-3un.13) with an optional sidecar file containing RaptorQ repair symbols. The main FSVI file is unchanged (backwards compatible), and the repair data lives in a parallel .fsvi.fec file.

SIDECAR FILE FORMAT (.fsvi.fec):

  Header (32 bytes, little-endian):
    Offset  Size  Field
    0       4     magic: "FECR" (FrankenSearch Erasure Code Repair)
    4       2     version: u16 (start at 1)
    6       2     reserved: u16
    8       4     symbol_size: u32 (bytes per symbol, e.g., 4096)
    12      4     k_source: u32 (number of source symbols)
    16      4     r_repair: u32 (number of repair symbols)
    20      4     overhead_percent: u32 (for documentation/verification)
    24      8     source_hash: u64 (xxh3_64 of the protected file)

  Repair Symbol Table (r_repair entries, each symbol_size bytes):
    [symbol_0: symbol_size bytes]
    [symbol_1: symbol_size bytes]
    ...
    [symbol_{r_repair-1}: symbol_size bytes]

  Footer (8 bytes):
    0       4     trailer_crc32: u32 (CRC32 of entire file excluding this footer)
    4       4     magic_end: "RCEF" (reverse magic, end-of-file marker)

FILE NAMING CONVENTION:
  vector.fast.fsvi     -> vector.fast.fsvi.fec
  vector.quality.fsvi  -> vector.quality.fsvi.fec

PROTECTION WORKFLOW:

  pub struct FsviProtector {
      codec: RepairCodec,
  }

  impl FsviProtector {
      /// Generate repair symbols for an FSVI file and write the .fec sidecar
      pub fn protect(&self, fsvi_path: &Path) -> SearchResult<ProtectionResult>;

      /// Verify FSVI file integrity using the .fec sidecar
      /// Returns VerifyResult::Intact if file matches source_hash
      /// Returns VerifyResult::Corrupted with repair info if damaged
      pub fn verify(&self, fsvi_path: &Path) -> SearchResult<VerifyResult>;

      /// Attempt to repair a corrupted FSVI file using the .fec sidecar
      /// On success, overwrites the corrupted file with repaired data
      /// On failure, returns error (corruption exceeds repair capacity)
      pub fn repair(&self, fsvi_path: &Path) -> SearchResult<RepairResult>;

      /// Atomic protect: write .fec to temp file, then rename (crash-safe)
      pub fn protect_atomic(&self, fsvi_path: &Path) -> SearchResult<ProtectionResult>;
  }

  pub struct ProtectionResult {
      pub source_size: u64,
      pub repair_size: u64,
      pub overhead_ratio: f32,
      pub k_source: u32,
      pub r_repair: u32,
      pub encode_time: Duration,
  }

  pub struct RepairResult {
      pub bytes_corrupted: usize,
      pub symbols_repaired: usize,
      pub decode_time: Duration,
      pub source_hash_before: u64,  // xxh3_64 of corrupted data
      pub source_hash_after: u64,   // xxh3_64 of repaired data (should match original)
  }

INTEGRATION WITH VECTOR INDEX WRITER (bd-3un.13):
  VectorIndexWriter::finish() should optionally call FsviProtector::protect_atomic()
  when the 'durability' feature is enabled. The protection happens AFTER fsync of the
  main index file, ensuring the repair symbols cover the durable version.

  Updated finish() flow:
  1. Write all records to FSVI file
  2. fsync the FSVI file
  3. fsync the parent directory
  4. If durability feature enabled:
     a. Compute repair symbols from the fsynced file
     b. Write .fec sidecar (atomic: temp + rename)
     c. fsync the .fec file and parent directory
     d. Log protection result

AUTOMATIC REPAIR ON LOAD:
  VectorIndex::open() should optionally verify integrity and attempt repair:

  impl VectorIndex {
      pub fn open(path: &Path) -> SearchResult<Self> {
          // ... existing load logic ...

          #[cfg(feature = "durability")]
          if let Some(protector) = FsviProtector::try_new() {
              match protector.verify(path)? {
                  VerifyResult::Intact => { /* fast path, < 1ms */ }
                  VerifyResult::Corrupted { repairable: true, .. } => {
                      tracing::warn!("vector index corrupted, attempting repair");
                      protector.repair(path)?;
                      tracing::info!("vector index repaired successfully");
                      // Re-open from repaired file
                  }
                  VerifyResult::Corrupted { repairable: false, .. } => {
                      tracing::error!("vector index corrupted beyond repair capacity");
                      return Err(SearchError::IndexCorrupted { path: path.to_owned() });
                  }
              }
          }
      }
  }

EXAMPLE SIZES:
  | Index Size | Symbols (4KB) | Repair (20%) | .fec Size | Overhead |
  |-----------|---------------|--------------|-----------|----------|
  | 7.3MB     | 1,869         | 374          | 1.5MB     | 20.5%    |
  | 73MB      | 18,688        | 3,738        | 14.6MB    | 20.0%    |
  | 730MB     | 186,880       | 37,376       | 146MB     | 20.0%    |

